  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "This topic might have been discussed before, though I couldn't seem to find any similar titles. The closest one I found in terms of disabling components on avatars in worlds would be this one (but it only talks about avatar stations): https://feedback.vrchat.com/feature-requests/p/allow-world-creators-of-sdk3-worlds-to-disable-stations-on-avatars\n\nAs it says in the title, I think world creators should be given the ability to disable certain components on an avatar. More specifically, any use of a Collider (not trigger) or Light(s), as these have often been used to gain some sort of unfair advantage in game worlds, especially when it comes to speed and height.\n\nIt'd be very beneficial for such worlds to blacklist these kinds of components, since the world creator doesn't have to make additional changes in an attempt to remove this kind of advantage. Scripts that are written to monitor this type of cheating aren't perfect by any means, as there could be false-positives, player circumventions, or some strange edge-case that wasn't caught beforehand. It'd just be one less problem to worry about if we could control which avatar components are allowed in a world.\n\nI understand this might be a bit of a controversial feature for some people, since it would break intended functionality of some avatars. And I can understand that if you're in a private instance on your own or with others, why would it matter if you use an avatar like that at all? You're just there to have fun. But if automated saving/loading (or maybe even global leaderboards) ever become a built-in feature within VRChat, then it just seems unfair to the others that simply played by the rules.\n\nAlso, yes, I know: Playspace Mover is a thing that people use as well, but that isn't something that can be disabled. However, this one is a bit easier to mitigate and doesn't lift the player's capsule collider off the ground.",
